348322d1e.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n\nII. The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n\nIII. The function is continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1\\ne 1=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ab5c9a-741e-507f-8a99-07a9f7aa451c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339588} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf55990-23dc-5693-8dde-0dd252a432b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339663} {:answer/id #uuid "48fe273f-13eb-5f78-8272-5b9b3a2db061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339782} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2895ac-425c-5001-bb2a-284e8601acaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339796} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ab1df-0cb6-5919-a2be-e9cca71bcc58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a2895ac-425c-5001-bb2a-284e8601acaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339796} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf84d3cb-0dfa-513a-bcdd-3183ad8dee6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79464211-37d1-5cac-b164-284bb9d9308a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73105" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad3fb8a0cce2a6dac706d7876aca0a2339fe49c05cb7aa6ae54de86735ceafab.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. The function is left-continuous at $x=3$\n\nII. The function is right-continuous at $x=3$\n\nIII. The function is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=3,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=1=f(3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=3,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."}]}
